      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression (AP), we need to use the property of arithmetic sequences. Specifically, for a sequence \\(d_1, d_2, d_3, d_4\\) to be an AP, the difference between consecutive terms must be the same. That is, \\(d_2 - d_1 = d_3 - d_2 = d_4 - d_3\\).\n\nIn this problem, the common difference \\(d\\) is the difference between consecutive terms of the sequence. So we have:\n1. \\(a - 10 = \\frac{b - a}{1}\\)\n2. \\(b - a = \\frac{ab - b}{1}\\)\n3. \\(ab - b = \\frac{ab - a}{1}\\)\n\nThese three equations can be simplified to:\n1. \\(a - 10 = b - a \\implies 2a - b = 10\\)\n2. \\(b - a = ab - b \\implies ab - 2b = -a \\implies ab - 2b + a = 0\\)\n3. \\(ab - b = ab - a \\implies b - a = 0 \\implies b = a\\) or \\(ab = b\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: \\(a = b\\)**\nIf \\(a = b\\), then substituting \\(b\\) with \\(a\\) in the first equation, we get:\n\\[2a - a = 10 \\implies a = 10\\]\nThus, \\(a = 10\\) and \\(b = 10\\). So one pair is \\((10, 10)\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(b = a\\)**\nIf \\(b = a\\), then substituting \\(a\\) with \\(b\\) in the first equation, we get:\n\\[2b - a = 10 \\implies b - a = 10\\]\nThe second equation simplifies to:\n\\[ab - 2b = -a \\implies ab - 2b + a = 0 \\implies b(a - 2) + a = 0 \\implies b = \\frac{a}{2 + a}\\]\nNow, substituting \\(b = \\frac{a}{2 + a}\\) in \\(a - b = 10\\), we get:\n\\[a - \\frac{a}{2 + a} = 10 \\implies \\frac{a(2 + a) - a}{2 + a} = 10 \\implies \\frac{a^2 + 2a}{2 + a} = 10 \\implies a^2 + 2a = 10(2 + a) \\implies a^2 + 2a = 20 + 10a \\implies a^2 - 8a - 20 = 0\\]\nSolving the quadratic equation \\(a^2 - 8a - 20 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula \\(a = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\\[a = \\frac{8 \\pm \\sqrt{64 + 80}}{2} = \\frac{8 \\pm \\sqrt{144}}{2} = \\frac{8 \\pm 12}{2} \\implies a = 10 \\text{ or } -2\\]\nIf \\(a = 10\\), then \\(b = 10\\), and we already know this pair is \\((10, 10)\\).\nIf \\(a = -2\\), then \\(b = -2\\), so another pair is \\((-2, -2)\\).\n\n**Case 3: \\(ab = 0\\)**\nIf \\(ab = 0\\), then one of \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) must be zero. So we have two sub-cases:\n- If \\(a = 0\\), then \\(b(a - 10) = 10b \\implies 0 = 10 \\text{ or } -b = 1\\), giving \\(b = 1\\). So one pair is \\((0, 1)\\).\n- If \\(b = 0\\), then \\(a(b - 10) = ab \\implies 10a = 0 \\text{ or } a = 0\\), giving \\(a = 0\\). So another pair is \\((0, 0)\\).\n\nThus, the five pairs are \\((10, 10)\\), \\((-2, -2)\\), \\((0, 1)\\), and \\((0, 0)\\). Therefore, the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).\nThe number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) forms an arithmetic progression is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
