      "text": "Given the arithmetic progression $10, a, b, ab$, let $2$ terms be $a$ and $b$.\n$(b-a) = (ab - b) \\implies b (a-1) = 2$\n$(b - a) = (b - 10) \\implies b(a - 1) = 10$\nThus, $10$ and $2$ are the only solutions and the pairs are $$(2,12); \\quad (12,2)$$ \nThere are $\\boxed{2}$ pairs.\nTo determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the arithmetic progression relation:**\n   Since \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms must be constant. Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   a - 10 = ab - b \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b - a = ab - 10\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve for \\(a\\) in terms of \\(b\\):**\n   From the first equation:\n   \\[\n   a - 10 = ab - b \\implies a - ab = 10 - b \\implies a(1 - b) = 10 - b\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{10 - b}{1 - b}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(b\\) in terms of \\(a\\):**\n   From the second equation:\n   \\[\n   b - a = ab - 10 \\implies b - a = ab - 10 \\implies b(1 - a) = a - 10\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(a = \\frac{10 - b}{1 - b}\\) into this equation:\n   \\[\n   b\\left(1 - \\frac{10 - b}{1 - b}\\right) = \\frac{10 - b}{1 - b} - 10\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   b\\left(\\frac{1 - b - (10 - b)}{1 - b}\\right) = b\\left(\\frac{1 - b - 10 + b}{1 - b}\\right) = b\\left(\\frac{-9}{1 - b}\\right) = -\\frac{9b}{1 - b}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the right-hand side:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10 - b}{1 - b} - 10 = \\frac{10 - b - 10(1 - b)}{1 - b} = \\frac{10 - b - 10 + 10b}{1 - b} = \\frac{9b - 10}{1 - b}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Equate both sides:**\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{9b}{1 - b} = \\frac{9b - 10}{1 - b}\n   \\]\n   Since the denominators are the same, we equate the numerators:\n   \\[\n   -9b = 9b - 10 \\implies -18b = -10 \\implies b = \\frac{5}{9}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find \\(a\\) using \\(b = \\frac{5}{9}\\):**\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{10 - \\frac{5}{9}}{1 - \\frac{5}{9}} = \\frac{\\frac{90}{9} - \\frac{5}{9}}{\\frac{9}{9} - \\frac{5}{9}} = \\frac{\\frac{85}{9}}{\\frac{4}{9}} = \\frac{85}{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Verify the solution:**\n   Check if \\((2, 12)\\) and \\((12, 2)\\) satisfy the original arithmetic progression condition:\n   - For \\((2, 12)\\):\n     \\[\n     12 - 2 = 10 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 12(2) - 12 = 0 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\((12, 2)\\):\n     \\[\n     12 - 12 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2(12) - 2 = 22 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n     \\]\n\nThus, there are no valid pairs \\((a, b)\\) that satisfy the given arithmetic progression condition. Therefore, the number of such pairs is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
